Lexicon sentiment: lowercase and tokenize. Add +1 for positive words and -1 for negative words. If the previous word is
not/never/no, reverse the current word's score. Total > 0 is positive, < 0 negative, and 0 neutral. It handles simple 'not helpful'
phrases but not sarcasm or complex grammar.
Extractive summary: split into sentences, remove stop words, count important words, score sentences using those frequencies, and
select the highest-scoring sentence(s) in original order. It selects existing sentences.
A5.2 Basic Chatbot
Match pollutant keywords, station names and date phrases. Validate station/date, map station to station_id and run a parameterised
query:
SELECT AVG(pm25) FROM readings
WHERE station_id = ? AND reading_date = ?;
Reply with value, unit, date and valid-reading count, or say no data is available. Limitations: fixed vocabulary/date formats, spelling
errors, ambiguous questions and no human-like context understanding.
Alternative: Naive Bayes learns from labelled examples and can generalise beyond a fixed word list, but needs representative
training data. A pre-trained language model can understand context better but uses more memory and inference time. Lexicon
scoring is O(w) per complaint with small memory; model inference costs more. Evaluate accuracy on labelled local complaints.
