B4. Final Engineering Decision
B4.1 Sentiment Implementation
POSITIVE = {"good","clean","fast","helpful","improved"}
NEGATIVE = {"bad","dirty","slow","rude","smoke","broken"}
NEGATORS = {"not","never","no"}
def score(text):
    words = text.lower().replace("."," ").replace(","," ").split()
    total = 0
    for i, word in enumerate(words):
        value = 1 if word in POSITIVE else -1 if word in NEGATIVE else 0
        if i > 0 and words[i-1] in NEGATORS: value = -value
        total += value
    return total
def label(text):
    s = score(text)
    return "positive" if s > 0 else "negative" if s < 0 else "neutral"
for complaint in [
 "Roads are clean and the bus was fast.",
 "Smoke from the factory, air is bad.",
 "The staff were not helpful.",
 "Not bad, service improved."
]:
    print(f"{label(complaint):8} | {complaint}")
Expected labels: positive, negative, negative and positive. A failure example is 'Great, another day breathing smoke': sarcasm may
be labelled negative or positive depending on the lexicon and context. 'Not only slow but also helpful' can also be misread by the
simple negation rule.
