B4.3 Production Decision
For 112 stations reporting every 15 minutes, the network receives 112 × 4 = 448 readings/hour or 10,752/day. Use validated,
indexed SQLite for a modest single-machine deployment, with transactions and backups. If concurrent writers, availability or data
volume exceed SQLite's practical limits, migrate to PostgreSQL. Keep raw files for audit, store cleaned data in a canonical schema
and retain rejected rows with reasons.
Schedule ingestion, validate units/ranges, use parameterised inserts, and calculate daily/monthly summaries in SQL. For 50,000
complaints/month, lexicon scoring is cheap and explainable, but use manually labelled agreement results to decide. If accuracy is
low or context matters, train and validate a classifier or use a pre-trained model with privacy/cost controls.
Alternative summary: selecting the first k sentences is fast and needs little scoring memory, but can miss the main point later in the
complaint. Frequency-based extractive summarisation processes words in approximately O(w) time and stores word frequencies
proportional to vocabulary size. Compare both on the same 50 complaints.
